#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199349} :activity/id #uuid "bb75a331-340e-5e54-8ba7-0ecf1d12b81d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050702 :topic/id #uuid "60335377-93cb-55a0-a4d1-bbe5d78644b6" :topic/math-academy-id 4196} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199492 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. \n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass. \n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly. \n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g.$\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "6d1214d5-e0a9-5ffd-8b79-73c1b78305b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7782 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "375871bc-899c-5a27-b8eb-fd1041399f58" :step/math-academy-id 45039 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198422 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198064 :question/id #uuid "857b2e09-68cc-5fc4-b9d0-1f6a9d43fbaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12510"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "666d43f8-bf05-51b0-af1f-d89540f4c29c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431672}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Metric Units of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "2c48ca90-f579-5512-92f0-b7c2525cca1e" :step/math-academy-id 45088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198065 :question/id #uuid "8783fe1f-5b08-5862-9e3d-231ba39ad4e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e01d9e5b-d4e5-5e84-84c5-33f6678b53ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431673}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating Mass Conversions in Metric Units"} :step/id #uuid "8a0b7db5-0023-5d56-b54d-db2933fe9960" :step/math-academy-id 45090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198068 :question/id #uuid "0526feee-cfe1-5aa2-9de0-a37e7a162492" :question/math-academy-id "e-12516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3ec882e-9438-5f6a-bcb3-3baa7a871f56" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431671}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Customary Units of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "74eaf0de-fbc6-5a80-ab6d-c4b4cf46dec0" :step/math-academy-id 45096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198069 :question/id #uuid "16c47009-d38d-5957-bc2d-525483e18161" :question/math-academy-id "e-12517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e0296c8-9ada-5097-a967-f10fc498a9eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431669}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating Mass Conversions in Customary Units"} :step/id #uuid "1da9dd54-2e79-527a-b1e6-5c97bb442520" :step/math-academy-id 45097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199496 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**. \n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}.$\n\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}.$\n\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}.$\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." :tutorial/id #uuid "71c4ce6d-783d-5c7a-ac8e-015cc5bde4c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7798 :tutorial/title "Customary Units of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "ffc96bd1-e431-5266-84b4-0ab22768851f" :step/math-academy-id 45119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199514 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c656aea-de27-5e2c-9a0f-d9a229d4e0c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7944 :tutorial/title "The Prefix System for Metric Units"} :step/id #uuid "09d54f2a-9e5f-5ab1-828e-02e12c87f8aa" :step/math-academy-id 45704 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199354}}] :activity/title "Units of Mass" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198501}]}
